class Solution {

    private Map<Node, Node> map = new HashMap<>();

    public Node cloneGraph(Node node) {

        if (node == null) {
            return null;
        }

        // Already cloned
        if (map.containsKey(node)) {
            return map.get(node);
        }

        // Create clone
        Node clone = new Node(node.val);

        // Store original -> clone
        map.put(node, clone);

        // Clone all neighbors
        for (Node neighbor : node.neighbors) {

            clone.neighbors.add(cloneGraph(neighbor));
        }

        return clone;
    }
} 
//"I'll use DFS along with a HashMap. 
// The HashMap will maintain a mapping between each original node and its cloned node.
//  When I encounter a node, I first check whether it has already been cloned. 
// If it has, I return the existing clone. 
// Otherwise, I create a new node with the same value, store the mapping before processing its neighbors, and recursively clone all its neighbors. 
// This also handles cycles because an already visited node will be retrieved from the HashMap instead of being cloned again."

//Have I cloned this node?
       ↓
     YES → return existing clone
       ↓
      NO
       ↓
Create clone
       ↓
Put original → clone in HashMap
       ↓
Clone all neighbors
       ↓
Return clone


//space complexity: O(N) where N is the number of nodes in the graph. This is because we store a mapping for each node in the HashMap and also create a clone for each node.
//time complexity: O(N + E) where N is the number of nodes and E is the number of edges in the graph. This is because we visit each node and edge once during the DFS traversal.